// @expect-error Cannot compare 'long' with 'double', because a 'double' cannot represent every 'long' exactly, so a large value would compare wrongly. Write 'wide as double == ratio' if comparing them as 'double' values is close enough.
// @expect-error Cannot compare 'double' with 'long', because a 'double' cannot represent every 'long' exactly, so a large value would compare wrongly. Write 'ratio != wide as double' if comparing them as 'double' values is close enough.
// @expect-error Cannot compare 'ulong' with 'double', because a 'double' cannot represent every 'ulong' exactly, so a large value would compare wrongly. Write 'huge as double == ratio' if comparing them as 'double' values is close enough.
// @expect-error Cannot compare 'int' with 'float', because a 'float' cannot represent every 'int' exactly, so a large value would compare wrongly. Convert both sides to 'double', as in 'count as double == part as double'.
// @expect-error Cannot compare 'float' with 'uint', because a 'float' cannot represent every 'uint' exactly, so a large value would compare wrongly. Convert both sides to 'double', as in 'part as double == size as double'.
// @expect-error Cannot compare 'long' with 'float', because a 'float' cannot represent every 'long' exactly, so a large value would compare wrongly. Write 'wide as float == part' if comparing them as 'float' values is close enough.
// @expect-error Cannot compare 'char' with 'double', because a 'char' is a code point, a character's number in Unicode, not a quantity. Compare it with a character instead, as in 'letter == 'a''. Or, to compare its number, convert 'letter' to 'double', as in 'letter as double == ratio'.
// @expect-error Cannot compare 'float' with 'char', because a 'char' is a code point, a character's number in Unicode, not a quantity. Compare it with a character instead, as in 'letter != 'a''. Or, to compare its number, convert 'letter' to 'float', as in 'part != letter as float'.
// @expect-error Cannot compare 'int' with 'uint', because neither type has every value of the other: an 'int' can be negative, and a 'uint' can be larger than any 'int'. Convert both sides to 'long', as in 'count as long == size as long'.
// @expect-error Cannot compare 'ulong' with 'long', because neither type has every value of the other: a 'long' can be negative, and a 'ulong' can be larger than any 'long'. Write 'huge == wide as ulong' if 'wide' is never negative.
// @expect-error Cannot compare 'short' with 'char', because neither type has every value of the other: a 'short' can be negative, and a 'char' can be larger than any 'short'. Convert both sides to 'int', as in 'small as int == letter as int'.
// @expect-error Cannot compare 'int' with 'ulong', because neither type has every value of the other: an 'int' can be negative, and a 'ulong' can be larger than any 'int'. Write 'count as ulong != huge' if 'count' is never negative.
// @expect-error Cannot compare 'long?' with 'double', because a 'double' cannot represent every 'long' exactly, so a large value would compare wrongly. Check 'maybe' for null first ('if (maybe != null)'). Write 'maybe as double == ratio' if comparing them as 'double' values is close enough.
// @expect-error Cannot compare 'long?' with 'double?', because a 'double' cannot represent every 'long' exactly, so a large value would compare wrongly. Check 'maybe' and 'perhaps' for null first ('if (maybe != null && perhaps != null)'). Write 'maybe as double == perhaps' if comparing them as 'double' values is close enough.
// @expect-error '<' needs the same number type on both sides, but 'wide' has type 'long' and 'ratio' has type 'double'. A 'double' cannot represent every 'long' exactly, so a large value would compare wrongly. Write 'wide as double < ratio' if comparing them as 'double' values is close enough.
// @expect-error '>=' needs the same number type on both sides, but 'huge' has type 'ulong' and 'wide' has type 'long'. Neither type has every value of the other: a 'long' can be negative, and a 'ulong' can be larger than any 'long'. Write 'huge >= wide as ulong' if 'wide' is never negative.
// @expect-error '<' needs the same number type on both sides, but 'count' has type 'int' and 'huge' has type 'ulong'. Neither type has every value of the other: an 'int' can be negative, and a 'ulong' can be larger than any 'int'. Write 'count as ulong < huge' if 'count' is never negative.
// @expect-error '<' needs the same number type on both sides, but 'count' has type 'int' and 'size' has type 'uint'. Convert both sides to 'long', as in 'count as long < size as long'.
// @expect-error '<=' needs the same number type on both sides, but 'part' has type 'float' and 'letter' has type 'char'. Convert 'letter' to 'float', as in 'part <= letter as float'.
// @expect-error build failed with 19 problems
// Two numbers whose types do not convert to each other cannot be compared, for a reason that depends
// on the pair: a 'char' is a code point rather than a quantity, a floating-point type is not exact
// for every value of a wide integer type, and a signed and an unsigned type each have values the
// other lacks. The fix converts what can be converted exactly, and otherwise names the condition, for
// an ordering as for an equality.
compare(long wide, double ratio, ulong huge, int count, float part, uint size, char letter, short small,
        long? maybe, double? perhaps) -> int {
    int matches = 0;
    if (wide == ratio) {
        matches++;
    }
    if (ratio != wide) {
        matches++;
    }
    if (huge == ratio) {
        matches++;
    }
    if (count == part) {
        matches++;
    }
    if (part == size) {
        matches++;
    }
    if (wide == part) {
        matches++;
    }
    if (letter == ratio) {
        matches++;
    }
    if (part != letter) {
        matches++;
    }
    if (count == size) {
        matches++;
    }
    if (huge == wide) {
        matches++;
    }
    if (small == letter) {
        matches++;
    }
    if (count != huge) {
        matches++;
    }
    if (maybe == ratio) {
        matches++;
    }
    if (maybe == perhaps) {
        matches++;
    }
    if (wide < ratio) {
        matches++;
    }
    if (huge >= wide) {
        matches++;
    }
    if (count < huge) {
        matches++;
    }
    if (count < size || part <= letter) {
        matches++;
    }
    return matches;
}

main() -> int {
    return compare(1, 1.0, 1, 1, 1.0f, 1, 'a', 1, null, null);
}
